Sure! To create a spy instance in the Elasticsearch test framework you’ll want to call the `SecureMockMaker_createSpy` function with your mock settings, handler, and the target object. Here’s the exact call you’d make (assuming you’ve already converted each argument into its Java‑string representation):

```json
{
  "settings": "<your mock creation settings as a Java object string>",
  "handler": "<your mock handler as a Java object string>",
  "object": "<the object you want to spy on, as a Java object string>"
}
```

In practice, if you’re writing this in Java code, it would look something like:

```java
String settings = /* your mock creation settings */;
String handler = /* your mock handler */;
String object = /* the object to spy on */;

Object spyInstance = functions.SecureMockMaker_createSpy(
    new HashMap<String, String>() {{
        put("settings", settings);
        put("handler", handler);
        put("object", object);
    }}
);
```

If you can share the actual string representations of `mockSettings`, `mockHandler`, and `testObject`, I can plug them in and give you the exact JSON payload to send to the tool.
